# Parsers and combinators

A grammar written with this library is a set of Avin values joined by operators that read like the
grammar itself:

```avin
val sum = chainl1(wholeNumber ^^ (s -> s.toLongOption().getOrElse(0)), literal("+") ^^^ ((a: long, b: long) -> a + b))
parseAll(sum, "1 + 2 + 3")          // [1:10] parsed: 6
```

`p ~ q` is `p` then `q`, `p | q` is `p` or else `q`, and `p ^^ f` turns what `p` read into a value.
This file defines the parser itself, every way of joining parsers, and how a parse is run. The
parsers that read text directly, characters, literals, names, numbers and strings, are in
`leaves.lavin`; the input they read and the result a parse answers are in `input.lavin`.

    module dev.avinlang.parsing

## What a parser is

A parser is a function from an `Input` to an `Option`. It answers `Some(value)` having moved the
input's offset past what it read, or `None` having recorded at the input what it expected. That is
the whole contract, and anything that keeps it is a parser: the library's own combinators are
written exactly as a user's custom parser would be.

`Parser` is a class rather than a bare function type so that the combinators can be its methods,
and so that `p | q` can be written as an operator. It is covariant: a `Parser[Dog]` is a
`Parser[Animal]`. A parser may have a name, given by `named`, which is how it shows.

    // A parser of a `T`. `run` reads from the input's offset; a custom parser is made by giving one.
    class Parser[+T](val run: Input -> Option[T], val name: string = "")

    impl[T] Display for Parser[T]
        display(out: Writer, spec: FormatSpec) = out.write(s"Parser ($name)")

## Sequence

The combinators are methods of an `impl` of `Parser[T]`. Methods named by symbols bind by their
first character, so every name with a `~` in it binds tightest, then `^^`, `^^^` and `^?`, then `|`
and `|||`. A rule like `a ~ b ^^ f | c` therefore needs no parentheses: it is `((a ~ b) ^^ f) | c`.

Every parser a combinator is given, other than the one it is a method of, is taken **by name**:
`p ~ q` does not read `q` when it is called, but the first time the parser it makes runs, and keeps
it. That is what lets a grammar refer to itself. Module-level rules are `lazy val`s, each made the
first time it is read, and making `expr` reads `atom`, which mentions `expr` again before it is
complete. Because `atom` only mentions it, as an operand that is read later, the cycle is never
followed while the rules are being made:

```avin
lazy val expr: Parser[long] = chainl1(term, addOp)
lazy val atom: Parser[long] = number | literal("(") ~> expr <~ literal(")")
```

The one place a rule is read at once is the receiver of a method: in `p | q`, `p` is made when the
choice is. A rule that begins with itself, `expr ~ literal("+") ~ term`, loops for ever when run in
any case, so a grammar never needs to.

`p ~ q` runs `p`, then `q` where `p` stopped, and pairs their values. If either fails the pair
fails, and the failing parser has already recorded what it expected.

    impl[T] Parser[T]
        // This parser, then `next`: both values.
        ~[U](next: -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[(T, U)]
            val q = later(next)
            Parser(input ->
                run(input) match
                    Some(a) -> q.run(input) match
                        Some(b) -> Some((a, b))
                        None -> None
                    None -> None)

Much of a grammar is punctuation whose value nobody wants: the brackets around a list, the colon
after a key. The arrow points at the side that is kept: `p ~> q` keeps `q`'s value and `p <~ q`
keeps `p`'s, so `literal("[") ~> items <~ literal("]")` is the items alone.

        // This parser, then `next`, whose value is kept.
        ~>[U](next: -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[U]
            val q = later(next)
            Parser(input ->
                run(input) match
                    Some(_) -> q.run(input)
                    None -> None)

        // This parser, then `next`, whose value is dropped.
        <~[U](next: -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[T]
            val q = later(next)
            Parser(input ->
                run(input) match
                    Some(a) -> q.run(input) match
                        Some(_) -> Some(a)
                        None -> None
                    None -> None)

`p ~! q` is `p ~ q` with a **commit point** between them. Once `p` has succeeded the grammar knows
which rule it is in, so a failure of `q` sets the input's `committed` flag, and every alternative
above it gives up instead of trying another branch from the start. The parse then answers an
`Error` rather than a `Failure`. After `literal("let") ~! ident`, a missing name is reported as the
name `let` wanted, rather than as whatever the next kind of statement would have begun with.

        // This parser, then `next`, committed: once this parser has succeeded, a failure of `next` fails
        // the whole parse instead of letting an alternative be tried.
        ~![U](next: -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[(T, U)]
            val q = later(next)
            Parser(input ->
                run(input) match
                    Some(a) -> q.run(input) match
                        Some(b) -> Some((a, b))
                        None ->
                            input.committed = true
                            None
                    None -> None)

## Choice

`p | q` tries `p`, and when it fails tries `q` **from where `p` started**, however far `p` had read.
Backtracking is a single assignment, since the offset is the only thing a parser moves. A
committed failure is not retried.

        // This parser, or `other` from the same place when this one fails before a commit point.
        |(other: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T]
            val q = later(other)
            Parser(input ->
                val start = input.offset
                run(input) match
                    Some(a) -> Some(a)
                    None ->
                        if input.committed then return None
                        input.offset = start
                        q.run(input))

`p ||| q` tries both from the same place and keeps whichever read further: the longest match, for
alternatives that share a beginning and that no order of `|` gets right, such as `<` and `<=`
written as separate rules. Where both read as far, `p` wins. Where only one succeeds, it is that
one, and where both fail the parse fails with what both expected.

        // This parser or `other`, from the same place, whichever reads further; this one when both read
        // as far.
        |||(other: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T]
            val q = later(other)
            Parser(input ->
                val start = input.offset
                val a = run(input)
                if input.committed then return None
                val endA = input.offset
                input.offset = start
                val b = q.run(input)
                if input.committed then return None
                a match
                    Some(x) -> b match
                        Some(y) if input.offset > endA -> Some(y)
                        _ ->
                            input.offset = endA
                            Some(x)
                    None -> b)

## Values

`p ^^ f` applies `f` to what `p` read; `map` is the same under a name. `p ^^^ v` replaces it with a
fixed value, which is how a keyword becomes a constant: `literal("null") ^^^ JNull`.

        // The value given to `f`.
        ^^[U](f: T -> U) -> Parser[U] = map(f)

        // The value given to `f`.
        map[U](f: T -> U) -> Parser[U] = Parser(input ->
            run(input) match
                Some(a) -> Some(f(a))
                None -> None)

        // `value` in place of what was read.
        ^^^[U](value: U) -> Parser[U] = Parser(input ->
            run(input) match
                Some(_) -> Some(value)
                None -> None)

`p ^? f` filters and maps in one step: `f` is a `PartialFunction`, and where it is not defined at
the value the parser fails, back where it started so that an alternative can try the same text. A
`match` with nothing before it, written where a partial function is expected, is one:
`wholeNumber ^? (match s if s.byteLength() <= 3 -> s.toIntOption().getOrElse(0))`. The second form says what
was wanted, given the value that was refused; the first says `value the function is defined at`.
With two arguments it is called with a dot: `p.^?(f, n -> s"$n is out of range")`.

        // The value given to `f`, where `f` is defined at it; a failure where this parser started,
        // expecting `value the function is defined at`, elsewhere.
        ^?[U](f: PartialFunction[T, U]) -> Parser[U] = self.^?(f, a -> "value the function is defined at")

        // The value given to `f`, where `f` is defined at it; a failure where this parser started,
        // expecting `error` of the value, elsewhere.
        ^?[U](f: PartialFunction[T, U], error: T -> string) -> Parser[U]
            val g = f.lift()
            Parser(input ->
                val start = input.offset
                run(input) match
                    Some(a) -> g(a) match
                        Some(b) -> Some(b)
                        None ->
                            input.offset = start
                            input.fail(error(a))
                    None -> None)

`flatMap` lets what was read choose what is read next: a closing tag that must repeat the opening
one, a length followed by that many items. `into` and `>>` are the same under the names a grammar
reads best with: `ident into (open -> ... literal(open))`, or `ident >> (open -> ... literal(open))`.
`>>` binds at the precedence of a shift, looser than `~` and `^^`.

        // The parser `f` makes of the value, run where this one stopped.
        flatMap[U](f: T -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[U] = Parser(input ->
            run(input) match
                Some(a) -> f(a).run(input)
                None -> None)

        // `flatMap(f)`.
        into[U](f: T -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[U] = flatMap(f)

        // `flatMap(f)`.
        >>[U](f: T -> Parser[U]) -> Parser[U] = flatMap(f)

`filter` refuses a value the grammar can read but does not accept: a name that is a keyword, a
number out of range. The refusal is reported where the value began, since that is where the text
went wrong, and the input is moved back there so an alternative can try the same text. It expects
`value the filter accepts`, which `withFailureMessage` replaces by something a reader understands:
`ident.filter(n -> !keywords.contains(n)).withFailureMessage("name that is not a keyword")`.
`withFilter` is the same, for a `for` with a guard.

        // The value when `p` accepts it; otherwise a failure where this parser started, expecting `value
        // the filter accepts`.
        filter(p: T -> bool) -> Parser[T] = Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            run(input) match
                Some(a) if p(a) -> Some(a)
                Some(_) ->
                    input.offset = start
                    input.fail("value the filter accepts")
                None -> None)

        // `filter(p)`.
        withFilter(p: T -> bool) -> Parser[T] = filter(p)

## Names and messages

`named` gives a parser the name it shows as, for a grammar's own debugging. It changes nothing about
what is parsed or reported.

        // This parser, showing as `Parser (n)`.
        named(n: string) -> Parser[T] = Parser(run, n)

`withFailureMessage` replaces what a failure says, as `input.lavin` explains: a rule that fails
without getting past its first character is reported as expecting `msg`, in place of everything its
parts expected. A failure further in keeps its own expectations, which say more. The first
character is the one after any white space the grammar skips there, since a rule that begins with a
token skips that first; for a rule that begins with a character parser, it is the one where the
rule began.
`withErrorMessage` does the same for a failure past a commit point, wherever it happened, since
such a failure is final.

        // This parser, reported as expecting `msg` when it fails without getting past its first
        // character, in place of the expectations of the parsers it is made of.
        withFailureMessage(msg: string) -> Parser[T] = Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            input.handleWhiteSpace()
            val token = input.offset
            input.offset = start
            val markStart = input.expectationsAt(start)
            val markToken = input.expectationsAt(token)
            val r = run(input)
            if r is None && !input.committed
                input.relabel(start, markStart, msg)
                if token != start then input.relabel(token, markToken, msg)
            r)

        // This parser, reported as expecting `msg` when it fails past a commit point.
        withErrorMessage(msg: string) -> Parser[T] = Parser(input ->
            val r = run(input)
            if r is None && input.committed then input.reword(msg)
            r)

## Repetition

A repetition is not something a parser does but something made of one, so it takes the parser it
repeats: `rep(statement)`, `repsep(value, literal(","))`.

`rep` runs a parser as many times as it succeeds. One case needs care: a parser that can succeed
without reading anything, such as `opt(literal("-"))`, would succeed for ever at the same place. So a run that reads nothing ends the repetition, and its value is not added. The loop itself
is `repeated`, below, shared by `rep` and `rep1`.

    // The values of `p` run as many times as it succeeds, none included. A run that reads nothing
    // ends the repetition without adding its value, so a parser that can succeed on nothing does not
    // loop.
    rep[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[List[T]]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input -> repeated(q, input, Buffer[T]()))

    // As `rep`, at least once.
    rep1[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[List[T]]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input -> atLeastOnce(q, q, input))

`rep1(first, p)` reads one `first`, then `p` as many times as it succeeds: a name whose first
character is a letter and whose others may be digits too.

    // One `first`, then the values of `p` as many times as it succeeds.
    rep1[T](first: -> Parser[T], p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[List[T]]
        val f = later(first)
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input -> atLeastOnce(f, q, input))

    // One `first`, then `p` as many times as it succeeds.
    private atLeastOnce[T](first: Parser[T], p: Parser[T], input: Input) -> Option[List[T]]
        val items = Buffer[T]()
        items.addOne(first.run(input)?)
        repeated(p, input, items)

`repN(n, p)` reads exactly `n` values: a count read earlier, a fixed shape such as the four digits
of an escape. It fails if `p` fails before the `n`th, and reads nothing after it.

    // Exactly `num` values of `p`.
    repN[T](num: int, p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[List[T]]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            val items = Buffer[T]()
            for _ in 0..<num
                items.addOne(q.run(input)?)
            Some(items.toList()))

`repsep` reads values with separators between them. A separator promises another value, so
`[1, 2,]` fails at the `]` rather than quietly accepting the trailing comma. The loop is
`separated`, below.

    // The values of `p` separated by `q`, none included. A separator must be followed by a value,
    // and a separator and value that together read nothing end the repetition.
    repsep[T, S](p: -> Parser[T], q: -> Parser[S]) -> Parser[List[T]]
        val v = later(p)
        val s = later(q)
        Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            v.run(input) match
                Some(a) -> separated(v, s, input, a)
                None ->
                    if input.committed then return None
                    input.offset = start
                    Some(List.empty[T]()))

    // As `repsep`, at least one value.
    rep1sep[T, S](p: -> Parser[T], q: -> Parser[S]) -> Parser[List[T]]
        val v = later(p)
        val s = later(q)
        Parser(input ->
            v.run(input) match
                Some(a) -> separated(v, s, input, a)
                None -> None)

Both loops have the same shape: remember where the next run starts, run, and on failure go back
there and stop. They stop also when a run read nothing. A committed failure is not a stop but a
failure of the whole repetition.

    // `p` run as many times as it succeeds, added to `items`.
    private repeated[T](p: Parser[T], input: Input, items: Buffer[T]) -> Option[List[T]]
        loop
            val start = input.offset
            p.run(input) match
                Some(a) ->
                    if input.offset == start then break
                    items.addOne(a)
                None ->
                    if input.committed then return None
                    input.offset = start
                    break
        Some(items.toList())

In `separated` the separator is optional but the value after it is not. A separator that fails ends
the list where it began; a value that fails after a separator fails the list, which is what makes
a trailing separator an error. `?` returns the `None` at once.

    // `first`, then the values of `p` after each `sep`.
    private separated[T, S](p: Parser[T], sep: Parser[S], input: Input, first: T) -> Option[List[T]]
        val items = Buffer[T]()
        items.addOne(first)
        loop
            val start = input.offset
            if sep.run(input) is None
                if input.committed then return None
                input.offset = start
                break
            val a = p.run(input)?
            if input.offset == start then break
            items.addOne(a)
        Some(items.toList())

## Optional parts and commit points

`opt` always succeeds: with `Some` of the value when the parser does, and with `None`, having read
nothing, when it does not.

    // `Some` of the value of `p`, or `None` without reading anything when `p` fails.
    opt[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[Option[T]]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            q.run(input) match
                Some(a) -> Some(Some(a))
                None ->
                    if input.committed then return None
                    input.offset = start
                    Some(None))

`commit(p)` makes a failure of `p` final, as `~!` does for what follows its left side: the parse
answers an `Error` naming what `p` expected.

    // `p`, whose failure fails the whole parse.
    commit[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            val r = q.run(input)
            if r is None then input.committed = true
            r)

## Positions

A syntax tree that reports errors of its own later, a type checker's "not defined" at the name's
line and column, keeps where each node began. A node type says so by conforming to `Positional`,
and that is all it writes: the trait holds the position itself, a field every conforming class
stores, which starts unset and which the parser sets:

```avin
class Name(val text: string): Positional

val n = Name("x")
n.pos                       // None until a parser, or the program, sets it
n.setPos(Position(0, 1, 1))
n.pos                       // Some(1:1)
```

A trait holding a field is conformed to by a class or an object, so a node type that wants a
position is a class rather than a `data` type.

`setPos` sets the position only while there is none. A value that already has one, such as a node
a rule built by reusing another, keeps it, so the position is that of the first rule to place the
value. Assigning `pos` directly replaces it, for a program that means to.

    // A value that remembers where in the text it began.
    trait Positional
        // Where the value began, or `None` while no position has been set.
        var pos: Option[Position] = None

        // Records where the value began, unless it already has a position.
        setPos(at: Position) = if pos.isEmpty() then pos = Some(at)

`positioned(p)` is `p` with its value's position set to where `p` began. It skips the grammar's
white space first, so the position is the value's own and not that of the space before it.

    // The value of `p`, positioned where `p` starts after any white space, unless it already has a
    // position.
    positioned[T: Positional](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            input.handleWhiteSpace()
            val start = input.offset
            q.run(input) match
                Some(a) ->
                    if a.pos.isEmpty() then a.setPos(input.position(start))
                    Some(a)
                None -> None)

## Operators and their grouping

An expression grammar is mostly levels of binary operators. Written as plain repetition each level
would yield a list for the user to fold; `chainl1` folds as it reads. Its operator parser yields the
function that combines two values, so one parser can stand for several operators:
`op("+", add) | op("-", subtract)`.

`chainl1` groups from the left, as subtraction and division do: `1 - 2 - 3` is `(1 - 2) - 3`. An
operator with no operand after it fails the chain, since the operator promised one. The second
form begins with a different parser, `first`, and folds values of another type into it: a call
followed by any number of arguments lists, a value followed by `.field` selections.

    // One `p` or more separated by `q`, combined from the left by the functions `q` yields:
    // `1 - 2 - 3` is `(1 - 2) - 3`.
    chainl1[T](p: -> Parser[T], q: -> Parser[(T, T) -> T]) -> Parser[T]
        val v = later(p)
        val o = later(q)
        Parser(input -> leftFolded(v, v, o, input))

    // One `first`, then any number of `p` each after a `q`, combined from the left by the functions
    // `q` yields.
    chainl1[T, U](first: -> Parser[T], p: -> Parser[U], q: -> Parser[(T, U) -> T]) -> Parser[T]
        val f = later(first)
        val v = later(p)
        val o = later(q)
        Parser(input -> leftFolded(f, v, o, input))

    // `first`, then `p` after each `q`, folded from the left.
    private leftFolded[T, U](first: Parser[T], p: Parser[U], q: Parser[(T, U) -> T], input: Input) -> Option[T]
        var acc = first.run(input)?
        loop
            val start = input.offset
            val g = q.run(input) match
                Some(g) -> g
                None ->
                    if input.committed then return None
                    input.offset = start
                    break
            acc = g(acc, p.run(input)?)
        Some(acc)

`chainr1` groups from the right, and folds into a value of a type of its own. It reads
`x₀ q₁ x₁ q₂ x₂ … qₙ xₙ` and answers `combine(x₀, f₁(x₁, f₂(x₂, … fₙ(xₙ, first))))`, where each
`fᵢ` is what `qᵢ` yielded. `first` is the value the fold starts from, and `combine` joins the first
value to the rest. A list built from the right is the plain case: `chainr1(item, literal(",") ^^^
cons, cons, Nil)`. Exponentiation is another, with `first` its identity, the exponent 1:
`chainr1(atom, literal("^") ^^^ power, power, 1)` reads `2 ^ 3 ^ 2` as `2 ^ (3 ^ (2 ^ 1))`.

It can fold only once it has everything, so it collects the values and functions as it reads and
folds them from the end. It does this in a loop rather than by recursion, so a long chain costs no
stack.

    // One `p` or more separated by `q`, folded from the right into `first` by the functions `q`
    // yields, the first value joined to the fold by `combine`.
    chainr1[T, U](p: -> Parser[T], q: -> Parser[(T, U) -> U], combine: (T, U) -> U, first: U) -> Parser[U]
        val v = later(p)
        val o = later(q)
        Parser(input ->
            val values = Buffer[T]()
            val fs = Buffer[(T, U) -> U]()
            values.addOne(v.run(input)?)
            loop
                val start = input.offset
                val g = o.run(input) match
                    Some(g) -> g
                    None ->
                        if input.committed then return None
                        input.offset = start
                        break
                fs.addOne(g)
                values.addOne(v.run(input)?)
            var acc = first
            var i = fs.length() - 1
            while i >= 0
                acc = fs(i)(values(i + 1), acc)
                i -= 1
            Some(combine(values(0), acc)))

## Parsers of nothing

`success` reads nothing and yields a value; `failure` reads nothing and fails, expecting `msg`; `err`
does the same past a commit point, so no alternative is tried after it and the parse answers an
`Error`. They are the ends of a choice: a default at the end of a list of alternatives, a branch of
`flatMap` that refuses, a rule that recognises a mistake and reports it as final.

    // Succeeds with `v`, reading nothing.
    success[T](v: T) -> Parser[T] = Parser(input -> Some(v))

    // Fails, expecting `msg`.
    failure[T](msg: string) -> Parser[T] = Parser(input -> input.fail(msg))

    // Fails, expecting `msg`, past a commit point: no alternative is tried after it.
    err[T](msg: string) -> Parser[T] = Parser(input ->
        input.expect(msg)
        input.committed = true
        None)

## Looking ahead

Two parsers look at the text without reading it. `guard(p)` succeeds where `p` does, with its
value, and leaves the offset where it was.

    // The value of `p`, without reading anything: a look ahead.
    guard[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            val r = q.run(input)
            input.offset = start
            r)

`not(p)` succeeds where `p` fails: `if` followed by a letter is the start of a name like
`iffy`, not the keyword. It restores the commit flag too, since a commit inside a look ahead commits
nothing outside it, and nothing `p` expects is recorded: in `if)`, the letter that would have made
`if` a name is no part of what the text lacks.

    // Succeeds without reading anything where `p` fails, and fails where `p` succeeds.
    not[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[unit]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            val start = input.offset
            val committed = input.committed
            input.lookingAhead += 1
            val r = q.run(input)
            input.lookingAhead -= 1
            input.offset = start
            input.committed = committed
            if r is None then Some(()) else None)

## Tracing

`log(p, name)` runs `p` and prints what happened: where it was tried, and whether it succeeded and
where it stopped. Wrapped around the rules of a grammar that does not do what its author expected, it
shows the order the rules ran in.

    // `p`, printing `trying name at line:column` before it runs and what it answered after.
    log[T](p: -> Parser[T], name: string) -> Parser[T]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            println(s"trying $name at ${input.pos()}")
            val r = q.run(input)
            if r is None then println(s"$name --> failed") else println(s"$name --> parsed up to ${input.pos()}")
            r)

## Running a parser

`phrase(p)` is `p` that must read the whole text, white space at the end included. A parser that
succeeds but stops short of the end has not parsed the text, so that is a failure expecting `end of
input`, recorded alongside whatever else could have continued there. For `1 + 2 )` the error says
the parser expected an operator or the end, and found `)`.

    // The value of `p`, which must read to the end of the text.
    phrase[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            val a = q.run(input)?
            input.handleWhiteSpace()
            if input.atEnd() then Some(a) else input.fail("end of input"))

A grammar is run by `parse` or `parseAll`. `parse` reads as much as the parser takes, for text that
goes on in some other language: a number at the start of a line, a header before a body. Its
result's `next()` says where it stopped. `parseAll` is `parse` of `phrase(p)`, and is how a whole
text is read.

Both skip white space before each token, by `literal`, the token parsers and `positioned`, as a
grammar of tokens usually wants. What white space is, and whether any is skipped, belongs to the
grammar, so it is set by a grammar that is a `RegexParsers`:

```avin
// Comments run from `#` to the end of the line, and count as white space.
object Config: RegexParsers
    override whiteSpace() -> Parser[unit] = rep(elem("white space", c -> c.isWhitespace()) ^^^ () | literal("#") ~ rep(elem("comment", c -> c != '\n')) ^^^ ()) ^^^ ()

    lazy val entry: Parser[(string, string)] = ident ~ (literal("=") ~> ident)

main()
    println(Config.parseAll(rep(Config.entry), "a = b  # one\nc = d"))
```

`whiteSpace()` is the parser skipped, white space in any script unless the grammar says otherwise,
and `skipWhitespace()` whether anything is skipped at all; a grammar that overrides it to `false`
reads every character itself. The grammar's `parse` and `parseAll` start the parse with that white
space, and every parser that skips it skips it for that parse. The `parse` and `parseAll` of the
module are those of a grammar that keeps both defaults.

    // A grammar of tokens: what white space is, whether it is skipped, and how a text is parsed with
    // it.
    trait RegexParsers
        // The white space skipped before each token: white space in any script.
        whiteSpace() -> Parser[unit] = spaces

        // Whether white space is skipped before each token.
        skipWhitespace() -> bool = true

        // The value of the longest beginning of `in` that `p` reads; the result's `next()` is where it
        // stopped.
        parse[T](p: Parser[T], in: string) -> ParseResult[T]
            val input = Input(in, if skipWhitespace() then Some(whiteSpace()) else None)
            input.result(p.run(input))

        // The value of the whole of `in`: `parse` of `phrase(p)`.
        parseAll[T](p: Parser[T], in: string) -> ParseResult[T] = parse(phrase(p), in)

    // The grammar whose white space is the default.
    private object DefaultParsers: RegexParsers

    // The value of the longest beginning of `in` that `p` reads, skipping white space in any script
    // before each token; the result's `next()` is where it stopped.
    parse[T](p: Parser[T], in: string) -> ParseResult[T] = DefaultParsers.parse(p, in)

    // The value of the whole of `in`, skipping white space in any script before each token and at the
    // end.
    parseAll[T](p: Parser[T], in: string) -> ParseResult[T] = DefaultParsers.parseAll(p, in)

## Recursive grammars

Every combinator takes its operands by name, as described under Sequence, and `later` is how it
keeps one: a parser that evaluates `p` the first time it runs, long after every rule is complete,
and keeps what it found. A by-name parameter is evaluated again at each read, so without `later` a
rule would be made anew at every step of every parse.

    // The parser `p` names, found the first time it runs rather than where `later` is called.
    private later[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T]
        var made: Option[Parser[T]] = None
        Parser(input ->
            val q = made match
                Some(q) -> q
                None ->
                    val q = p
                    made = Some(q)
                    q
            q.run(input))

Each level of nesting is a few calls deep, so how deep a text can nest is bounded by the stack of
the thread parsing it. An arithmetic grammar takes a few thousand levels of parentheses on the main
thread, and a parse run on a thread spawned with a larger stack goes as deep as that stack allows.
